SAT_CHUNK("rw/inferences/2/1",[
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e7e5b92714","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-0","passage":"<p>For decades, farmers in one river valley burned the stubble left in their rice fields after each harvest. Burning was fast and cheap, but it filled the air with smoke for weeks. When a local cooperative offered to buy the stubble instead, many farmers were skeptical that anyone would want it. Within three years, however, the cooperative was turning the straw into packaging material and selling it to companies across the region, and the autumn smoke had largely disappeared.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, why were many farmers initially skeptical of the cooperative's offer?","choices":["They doubted that there was any demand for rice stubble.","They believed that burning stubble improved the soil.","They worried that selling stubble was illegal.","They thought the cooperative would pay too little."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The text says they were skeptical \"that anyone would want it.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9ebaae351f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-1","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Mrs. Achebe had taught piano in the same small room for forty years. The upright piano had a cracked key and a pedal that squeaked, and every student eventually asked why she did not replace it. She always gave the same answer: a perfect instrument forgives too much. On this one, she said, you have to listen.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about Mrs. Achebe's students?","choices":["They tend to notice the piano's flaws.","They think the piano has an unusually beautiful sound.","They rarely practice outside of lessons.","They are mostly adults."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Every student eventually asked why she did not replace it,\" so they notice the cracked key and squeaky pedal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.94e6e6a5ed","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-7","passage":"<p>In 1928, the town of Eastbridge replaced its wooden footbridge with a concrete one. The new bridge was stronger, but residents soon noticed that the swallows that had nested beneath the old bridge for generations were gone. The birds' mud nests could not cling to the smooth concrete. In 2019, volunteers attached rough wooden panels to the underside of the bridge. The following spring, the swallows returned.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the volunteers most likely chose rough wooden panels because","choices":["the swallows' mud nests could cling to a rough surface.","wood is less expensive than concrete.","rough panels made the bridge stronger.","the town wanted to restore the original wooden bridge."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The nests \"could not cling to the smooth concrete,\" so a rough surface would help. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2df3c2f54e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-8","passage":"<p>Economists sometimes describe a \"cobra effect,\" in which a policy produces the opposite of its intended result. The term comes from a story, possibly exaggerated, about a reward once offered for dead cobras to reduce their numbers. Some residents reportedly began breeding cobras to collect the reward. When officials canceled the program, the breeders released their now-worthless snakes, leaving more cobras than before. Whether or not every detail is accurate, economists use the story to show how incentives can backfire.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which situation would an economist most likely describe as an example of the cobra effect?","choices":["A city lowers bus fares, and more people ride the bus.","A city pays people for each rat tail they turn in, and some people begin raising rats.","A town plants trees, and summer temperatures fall.","A school adds tutoring, and test scores rise."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Only this example shows an incentive producing more of the problem it was meant to reduce. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.51c6d01110","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-9","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a travel essay.</p><p>The market opens before the sun does. By five, vendors have unfolded their tables and arranged pyramids of oranges and bundles of herbs still wet with dew. By seven, the aisles are so crowded that shoppers move sideways. By noon it is over: the tables fold, the tarps come down, and a street cleaner sweeps away the last crushed petals. An hour later, no one walking by would guess that a thousand people had passed through that morning.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which statement about the market is most reasonable?","choices":["Its vendors leave their tables set up overnight.","It is popular despite operating for only a few hours.","It attracts very few customers on most days.","It sells mostly items that were imported from far away."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A thousand people pass through, yet it runs only from before dawn to noon. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1fea2b2d7d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-12","passage":"<p>Glass frogs, found in Central and South American rain forests, have translucent skin on their undersides. For years, scientists assumed this transparency simply helped the frogs blend in with the leaves they rest on. A recent study found something more surprising: while sleeping, a glass frog moves most of its red blood cells into its liver, making its body far more transparent. When the frog wakes and becomes active, the blood cells return to circulation.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a glass frog would most likely be least transparent when it is","choices":["hopping between leaves in search of food.","hiding with most of its blood stored in its liver.","resting motionless on the underside of a leaf.","sleeping on a leaf during the day."],"answer":"A","explanation":"When the frog is active, \"the blood cells return to circulation,\" reducing transparency. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d9c8825b07","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-13","passage":"<p>In the early years of the sport, marathon courses varied widely in length, from about 40 to 42 kilometers. Records set on one course could not be fairly compared with those set on another. In 1921, athletics officials adopted a standard distance of 42.195 kilometers, the length of a well-known course from a previous international competition. Only after that decision did marathon records become meaningful across different races.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, before 1921 a runner who set a record on a 40-kilometer course would most likely","choices":["not be fairly compared with a runner who set a record on a 42-kilometer course.","hold the official world record for the marathon.","be disqualified from future races.","be required to run the course again."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Records set on one course could not be fairly compared with those set on another.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fec11ce529","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-14","passage":"<p>A team of volunteers in a coastal town spent a summer recording every species of bird they saw along a single kilometer of shoreline. They expected to count perhaps thirty species. They counted seventy-two. Several were migrants resting for only a day or two, which the volunteers would have missed if they had visited less often. The team concluded that frequent, repeated observation reveals far more about a place than occasional surveys do.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a researcher who surveyed the same shoreline only once a month would most likely","choices":["record exactly seventy-two species.","record fewer species than the volunteers did.","record more migrant species than the volunteers did.","find that no migrants visit the shoreline."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Several migrants stayed only a day or two and would be missed by less frequent visits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d6ada7cb59","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-15","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather's garden was a map of his life. The fig tree by the gate came from a cutting he had carried across an ocean wrapped in a damp shirt. The roses along the fence had been a gift on the day my mother was born. And the stubborn patch of mint, which he pulled up every spring and which grew back every summer, he said reminded him of himself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grandfather most likely compares himself to the mint because he sees himself as","choices":["persistent in the face of repeated setbacks.","gentle and easily discouraged.","unable to grow in a new place.","difficult for others to understand."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The mint is \"stubborn\" and grows back every summer after being pulled up. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.911444c587","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-17","passage":"<p>Early mapmakers often filled the unexplored edges of their maps with drawings of sea monsters. Historians once treated these images as simple decoration or evidence of superstition. One historian has proposed a different reading: many of the creatures closely resemble descriptions from sailors' reports of whales, walruses, and giant squid. On this view, the drawings were mapmakers' attempts, however imperfect, to record real animals that travelers had described.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which finding would be most consistent with the historian's view?","choices":["A map's sea monster closely matches a sailor's written description of a walrus from the same period.","Sea monsters appear on maps only after whales had been scientifically described.","Mapmakers of the period described their drawings as purely imaginary.","A map's sea monster appears in an area where no sailor had traveled."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A close match between a drawing and a sailor's description supports the idea that the drawings recorded real animals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ddbcd3873e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-19","passage":"<p>Scientists studying tree rings from a forest in a dry region found that rings formed during years of heavy rainfall were noticeably wider than rings from drought years. By measuring rings in trees that were hundreds of years old, the team reconstructed a record of rainfall stretching back far earlier than any written weather records from the area. The reconstruction revealed several long droughts that had not previously been documented.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a series of unusually narrow rings in a tree most likely indicates","choices":["a mistake in the scientists' measurements.","a period of low rainfall.","a year when the tree was planted.","a period of heavy rainfall."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Rings from drought years were narrower than rings from wet years. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.82948331e5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-20","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father never told me he was proud of me. He was not a man who said such things. But every newspaper clipping that mentioned my name, even a single line about a spelling bee, ended up in a folder in his desk drawer. I found the folder after he died. It was two inches thick.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which statement about the narrator's father is most reasonable?","choices":["He rarely read the newspaper.","He valued her achievements even though he did not say so.","He kept the folder hidden because he was ashamed of it.","He wanted the narrator to become a journalist."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He saved every clipping, \"even a single line,\" despite never saying he was proud. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3903fac56d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-29","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, the carnival arrived in town on the same Tuesday and left on the same Sunday. Nadia had watched it come and go for eleven years without ever riding the Ferris wheel. This year, standing in line with a ticket that had cost her two weeks of saved allowance, she felt her heart pounding. When the attendant waved her forward, she stepped into the swaying seat before she could change her mind.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Nadia most likely stepped into the seat quickly because she","choices":["had ridden the Ferris wheel many times before.","wanted to get a better view of the attendant.","worried that she might lose her nerve if she hesitated.","was late for another ride."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She acted \"before she could change her mind,\" showing she feared losing her nerve. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.875f4c3be2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-31","passage":"<p>A composer who writes music for video games explained that her work differs from writing for film in one important way: in a film, every scene lasts exactly as long as the director decides, but in a game, the player controls how long each moment lasts. A player might spend thirty seconds or thirty minutes exploring a single room. As a result, she writes pieces that can loop smoothly or shift gradually to match what the player is doing.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a piece of game music that ended abruptly after one minute would most likely be a problem because","choices":["the player might remain in that part of the game for much longer.","film directors require music to end quickly.","players rarely spend more than a minute in any room.","games do not allow music to be repeated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A player \"might spend thirty seconds or thirty minutes\" in one room. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.df64553547","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-32","passage":"<p>Scientists once assumed that honeybees navigate mainly by remembering landmarks. Experiments have shown that bees also rely on the position of the sun, and they can compensate for the sun's movement across the sky over the course of a day. Even when clouds hide the sun, bees can detect patterns of polarized light in patches of blue sky, allowing them to keep their bearings.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if a bee were flying on a partly cloudy day with the sun hidden, it would most likely","choices":["still be able to orient itself using polarized light in visible patches of blue sky.","wait until the clouds cleared to begin flying.","rely only on the scent of flowers.","be unable to return to its hive."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Bees \"can detect patterns of polarized light in patches of blue sky.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1f5d993533","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-34","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century letter.</p><p>My dear sister, the voyage has been long, and the sea has not been kind. For nine days the waves rose higher than the mast, and I confess I did not expect to write to you again. Yet this morning the sky cleared, a flock of gulls circled the ship, and the captain says we shall see land within two days. I have never been so glad to hear the cry of a bird.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the writer was most likely glad to hear the gulls because","choices":["the captain had asked everyone to watch for birds.","the writer had never seen gulls before.","the gulls' cries meant another storm was coming.","birds near the ship suggested that land was not far away."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Gulls appear just as the captain says land is two days away. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0dc9efc9f2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-35","passage":"<p>Many cities have begun replacing streetlights with LED bulbs, which use far less electricity than older lamps. Some astronomers and ecologists have raised a concern: early LED streetlights emitted a large amount of blue light, which scatters widely in the atmosphere and can disrupt the behavior of nocturnal animals. Newer LED designs produce warmer light with less blue, offering energy savings with fewer side effects.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a city choosing new LED streetlights to minimize effects on nocturnal animals would most likely select lights that","choices":["use more electricity than older lamps.","emit warm light with relatively little blue.","are brighter than any previous streetlights.","emit as much blue light as possible."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Newer designs with less blue have \"fewer side effects.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.59671a2123","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-36","passage":"<p>When a regional airline began weighing passengers' carry-on bags at the gate, travelers complained about the delays. The airline explained that its small planes have strict weight limits, and knowing the total weight of luggage helps pilots calculate how much fuel to carry. Within a few months, passengers adjusted by packing lighter, and boarding times returned to normal.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, boarding times most likely returned to normal because","choices":["lighter bags made the weighing process quicker and caused fewer problems at the gate.","the airline hired more gate agents.","the airline switched to larger planes.","pilots stopped calculating fuel needs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passengers \"adjusted by packing lighter,\" and then boarding times returned to normal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.98dad093d5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-38","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>For three generations the Okonkwo family had run the hardware store on Main Street, and for three generations the bell above its door had rung the same flat note. When Adaeze took over the shop, she replaced the dusty shelves, repainted the walls a pale green, and installed bright new lights. But when a customer suggested she replace the bell, she shook her head. Some things, she said, should sound exactly the way they always have.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Adaeze most likely keeps the bell because she","choices":["does not notice the sound it makes.","cannot afford a new bell.","believes customers want a louder bell.","values its link to her family's long history with the store."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Some things ... should sound exactly the way they always have\" connects the bell to three generations. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f9a28413b5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-41","passage":"<p>Many people picture icebergs as white, but some are strikingly blue. The color comes from ice that formed under great pressure deep within a glacier. That pressure squeezes out the tiny air bubbles that normally scatter all colors of light and make ice look white. Without those bubbles, the dense ice absorbs more red light than blue, so blue light is what reaches our eyes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, ice that contains many tiny air bubbles would most likely appear","choices":["white.","transparent.","red.","blue."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Bubbles \"normally scatter all colors of light and make ice look white.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.df963af35d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-44","passage":"<p>In many cities, rooftop gardens are praised for cooling buildings and reducing stormwater runoff. A research team measuring rooftops in one city confirmed both benefits but found a third that residents had not expected: the gardens attracted dozens of species of bees, including several rarely seen elsewhere in the city. The researchers now argue that rooftop gardens should be designed with pollinators in mind, using flowering plants that bloom at different times of year.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers recommend plants that bloom at different times of year most likely because","choices":["bees avoid plants that bloom in summer.","such plants cool buildings more effectively.","such plants would provide food for bees across more of the year.","such plants are less expensive to maintain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Staggered blooming would support pollinators over a longer period. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1febbcb39d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-45","passage":"<p>For years, a small theater company performed classic plays in their original formal language. Attendance slowly declined. When a new director proposed staging the same plays with modern costumes and settings while keeping every original word, longtime members worried that the changes would distract from the text. Instead, audiences grew, and several reviewers wrote that the unfamiliar settings made them listen to the familiar words more closely than ever.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the longtime members' concern turned out to be","choices":["shared by most reviewers.","the reason attendance declined.","largely unfounded.","fully justified."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Reviewers said the settings helped them listen \"more closely than ever.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5f0058a5f0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-46","passage":"<p>Hummingbirds have one of the highest metabolic rates of any animal and must eat almost constantly while awake. At night, however, many species enter a state called torpor, lowering their body temperature and heart rate dramatically. A hummingbird in torpor can use a small fraction of the energy it would need to stay fully active overnight, allowing it to survive until it can feed again in the morning.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a hummingbird that could not enter torpor on a cold night would most likely","choices":["use far more energy overnight than a hummingbird in torpor.","need no food the next morning.","stop needing to eat during the day.","have a lower heart rate than usual."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Torpor uses \"a small fraction of the energy\" needed to stay active. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.18b10b3fff","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-48","passage":"<p>A psychologist asked participants to predict how happy they would feel a year after a major event, such as moving to a new city. Most predicted that their happiness would change a great deal, for better or worse. When the psychologist checked in a year later, most participants' happiness had returned close to their original levels. The study adds to evidence that people tend to overestimate how long the emotional effects of events will last.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a person who expects to feel miserable for years after failing a driving test would most likely","choices":["feel exactly as miserable as they predicted.","never recover from the disappointment.","feel happier than ever within a day.","feel better sooner than they expect."],"answer":"D","explanation":"People \"overestimate how long the emotional effects of events will last.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6a386d3193","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-49","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century travel diary.</p><p>We reached the pass at noon, and the guide told us to rest. I could not. Before me the valley opened like a book whose pages I had waited all my life to read: the river glinting silver, the villages no larger than thumbprints, and beyond them, rank upon rank, mountains I had known only from maps. My legs ached, but I stood until the guide took my arm and insisted that I sit.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, why did the guide most likely take the writer's arm?","choices":["To lead her back down the mountain.","To stop her from reading a book.","To make sure she rested after the tiring climb.","To point out a village in the valley."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The guide had told them to rest and \"insisted that I sit.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.89dec599b6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-50","passage":"<p>In one study, researchers placed identical bowls of candy on desks in an office, some in clear bowls and some in opaque bowls. Over several weeks, employees ate noticeably more candy from the clear bowls. When the researchers moved the clear bowls just two meters away from the desks, candy consumption dropped sharply. The findings suggest that small changes in how visible and convenient food is can significantly affect how much people eat.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, an office that wanted employees to eat more fruit would most likely benefit from","choices":["buying more expensive fruit.","storing fruit in opaque containers in a separate room.","removing all other snacks from the building.","placing fruit in clear bowls close to where people work."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Visible, convenient food was eaten more. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.140b178f44","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-53","passage":"<p>Early photographs often show people with stiff, serious expressions. It is tempting to conclude that people in the nineteenth century were more solemn than people today. But early cameras required subjects to stay perfectly still for many seconds, sometimes minutes, and holding a natural smile that long is difficult. Many portrait traditions of the time also associated dignity with a calm, composed face.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if early cameras had captured images in a fraction of a second, old photographs would most likely show","choices":["exactly the same stiff expressions for the same reasons.","more natural expressions, though portrait customs might still favor composed faces.","only people who were smiling.","no people at all."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Removing the long exposure removes one reason for stiffness, but customs remain. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2a58540bb5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-54","passage":"<p>A city once required new buildings to include large parking garages, reasoning that plenty of parking would reduce traffic on crowded streets. Urban researchers later studied the policy's effects. They found that abundant, cheap parking encouraged more people to drive downtown, adding to congestion, while the garages took up space that could have been used for housing or shops. After reviewing the research, the city eliminated the requirement in its central district.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the city's original reasoning about parking turned out to be","choices":["the reason more housing was built.","fully supported by the research.","the opposite of what the policy actually produced.","irrelevant to downtown traffic."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Officials expected less traffic, but parking \"encouraged more people to drive downtown.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fba84b23c7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-57","passage":"<p>Crows are known for their intelligence, but a study of city crows revealed a particularly surprising ability. Researchers wearing a distinctive mask trapped and released a few crows. For years afterward, crows in the area scolded and dive-bombed anyone wearing that mask, including crows that had never been trapped, while ignoring researchers in other masks. The birds had apparently learned to recognize a dangerous face and passed that knowledge to others.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if the researchers had trapped the crows while wearing no mask, the crows would most likely have","choices":["become unable to recognize any faces.","attacked every person in the city.","had a harder time identifying which people to avoid later.","stopped living in the area immediately."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The distinctive mask gave the crows a clear feature to recognize. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6c1efc497a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-58","passage":"<p>In the 1950s, engineers designing a new highway through a valley proposed cutting down an old grove of oak trees. Local residents objected, noting that the grove was among the last remnants of the forest that once covered the valley. After months of debate, the engineers shifted the highway's route slightly to the east. The adjustment added a few hundred meters to the road, but today the grove is a protected park crossed by walking trails.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the residents most likely valued the grove because","choices":["it had recently been planted by the town.","it provided the shortest route for the highway.","it was a rare surviving part of the valley's original forest.","it blocked the view of the new highway."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It was \"among the last remnants of the forest.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5add297981","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-59","passage":"<p>Children learning to read often sound out unfamiliar words letter by letter. Skilled adult readers, by contrast, recognize most familiar words instantly as whole units. Researchers studying eye movements have found, however, that even skilled readers slow down and fix their gaze longer on rare or unfamiliar words, suggesting that the older strategy of decoding piece by piece never disappears entirely but is used when needed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a skilled reader encountering the rare word \"sesquipedalian\" would most likely","choices":["skip the sentence entirely.","read the word faster than familiar words.","recognize it instantly without slowing down.","pause longer on the word than on common words."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Skilled readers \"fix their gaze longer on rare or unfamiliar words.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e5a05e4ba9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-60","passage":"<p>When a regional orchestra noticed that few young people attended its concerts, it began offering a free \"pay what you wish\" performance each month. Critics warned that people would come once for free and never buy full-price tickets. Two years of sales data showed a different pattern: many first-time attendees at the free concerts later returned to regular concerts, and the average age of subscribers dropped by nearly a decade.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the drop in subscribers' average age most likely indicates that","choices":["the free concerts were attended mostly by older people.","younger people who first attended free concerts went on to subscribe.","the orchestra lost all of its older subscribers.","subscriptions became more expensive."],"answer":"B","explanation":"First-time attendees returned, and subscribers became younger on average. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3b5dcfedf6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-62","passage":"<p>Many coastal towns once dumped the shells left over from seafood processing into landfills. In one bay, a restoration group began collecting oyster shells from restaurants and returning them to the water in mesh bags. Young oysters need hard surfaces to attach to, and the recycled shells gave them a place to settle. Within a few years, new reefs had formed, filtering the water and providing habitat for fish and crabs.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a bay with muddy bottoms and no hard surfaces would most likely","choices":["develop oyster reefs faster than other bays.","have clearer water than a bay with reefs.","attract more fish than a bay with reefs.","have difficulty supporting the growth of new oyster reefs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Young oysters \"need hard surfaces to attach to.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.b86f506100","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-65","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter had sat unopened on Tomas's desk for three days. He knew the university's decision was inside. Each morning he picked it up, weighed it in his hand as if its thickness could tell him the answer, and set it down again. On the fourth morning his little sister marched in, tore it open before he could stop her, and read the first line aloud with a shriek of delight.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Tomas most likely \"weighed it in his hand\" because he","choices":["planned to mail the letter to someone else.","needed to know how much postage it had required.","hoped the letter's size might reveal whether the news was good or bad.","wanted to see whether his sister had opened it."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"As if its thickness could tell him the answer.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.97680c31b7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-66","passage":"<p>Wind turbines are often placed on ridges and hilltops, where winds are strongest. Some wildlife biologists raised concerns that turbines in such locations could harm migrating birds of prey, which ride the same updrafts along ridges. A study tracking hawks and eagles near one wind farm found that collisions were concentrated at a few turbines positioned directly in the birds' main flight corridors. Shutting down those turbines during peak migration weeks reduced collisions sharply while cutting the farm's yearly energy production by less than one percent.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the solution was effective mainly because","choices":["every turbine was removed from the ridge.","the birds stopped migrating along the ridge.","the farm produced more energy during migration.","the collisions occurred at only a small number of identifiable turbines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Knowing where collisions occurred allowed a narrow, low-cost fix. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.45a2ab99db","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-68","passage":"<p>Sea levels were far lower during the last ice age, when enormous amounts of water were locked in glaciers. Areas that are now shallow seas were once dry land. Archaeologists have begun searching these submerged landscapes with sonar and divers, and they have found stone tools and the remains of hearths beneath the seafloor. Such discoveries suggest that some early human settlements now lie underwater, which may explain gaps in the archaeological record on land.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the \"gaps in the archaeological record on land\" most likely refer to","choices":["areas where glaciers never formed.","disagreements among archaeologists about sonar.","damaged tools stored in museums.","periods or places where evidence of human activity has been hard to find."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Missing evidence may lie underwater. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c33b331137","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-69","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every Saturday, Rafael's grandmother made tamales, and every Saturday she refused to write down the recipe. \"It's in my hands,\" she would say, holding them up, dusted with masa. So Rafael began standing beside her, watching how much she scooped, how long she kneaded, and how she folded each husk. Years later, in his own kitchen, he realized that the recipe had become his hands too.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grandmother's statement \"It's in my hands\" most likely means that","choices":["she wants Rafael to find a written recipe elsewhere.","she has hurt her hands while cooking.","her knowledge of the recipe comes from practice rather than written instructions.","she keeps the recipe hidden in her apron."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She knows it by feel and experience. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.085818b712","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-71","passage":"<p>When the price of a city's public transit passes was cut in half for residents over sixty-five, officials expected ridership among older residents to rise modestly. It nearly doubled. Surveys revealed that many older residents had wanted to use transit more but had been deterred by cost. Several also reported visiting friends, libraries, and parks more often, suggesting that the policy affected not only transportation but also social life.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, before the price cut, cost had most likely","choices":["caused older residents to buy cars.","kept some older residents from using transit as often as they wished.","encouraged older residents to ride transit more.","had no effect on how often older residents traveled."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Many \"had been deterred by cost.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.dbcdaa499d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-72","passage":"<p>Most spiders have eight eyes, but their eyesight varies widely. Web-building spiders often see poorly and sense prey mainly through vibrations in their silk. Jumping spiders, which hunt without webs, have large forward-facing eyes that give them some of the sharpest vision among small animals. This contrast suggests that a spider's hunting method is closely linked to how much it relies on sight.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a spider that stalks and pounces on insects would most likely","choices":["rely entirely on vibrations in silk.","build large webs.","have fewer than eight eyes.","have relatively sharp vision."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Hunting without webs is linked to sharp eyesight. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.418a0f0b8d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-73","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay about walking.</p><p>I used to measure a walk by its destination: the store, the office, the train. Then an injury took away my car for a season, and I began walking everywhere, slowly. I learned which porch held a sleeping cat, which corner smelled of bread at seven, which tree dropped its leaves first. The destinations were the same, but the walks had become places in themselves.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the details about the cat, the bread, and the tree mainly show that the writer","choices":["wanted to find a faster route.","had moved to a farm.","walked only early in the morning.","began noticing small features of the route."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They illustrate the attention gained by walking slowly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1d8590f9d4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-74","passage":"<p>Many people assume that the Great Pyramid's builders used slaves forced to work under harsh conditions. Excavations near the pyramid, however, have uncovered workers' villages with bakeries, breweries, and cemeteries where laborers were buried with bread and beer for the afterlife. Some skeletons show healed injuries that suggest the workers received medical care. These findings have led many archaeologists to conclude that the builders were paid laborers who were respected for their work.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, workers being buried with bread and beer most likely suggests that","choices":["bread and beer were rare in the region.","they did not work on the pyramid.","they were punished after death.","they were treated with a degree of respect."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Burial offerings indicate respect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fcb5d817ca","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-77","passage":"<p>Scientists once thought that the deep ocean floor was nearly lifeless, too dark and cold to support much activity. Then researchers exploring volcanic vents discovered thriving communities of giant tube worms, clams, and shrimp. These animals depend not on sunlight but on bacteria that draw energy from chemicals released by the vents. The discovery showed that life can flourish in conditions once considered impossible.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the bacteria at the vents are important to the other animals because they","choices":["keep the water warm.","block chemicals released by the vents.","protect the animals from predators.","provide a source of energy that does not depend on sunlight."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Animals depend on bacteria that \"draw energy from chemicals.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5f5ee29bc5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-78","passage":"<p>When a large bookstore chain opened near a small independent bookshop, many predicted that the smaller store would close within a year. Instead, the owner began hosting weekly author readings, a children's story hour, and a book club that met in the shop after hours. Sales of new books dipped slightly, but sales of books connected to events rose, and the shop celebrated its twentieth anniversary a decade later.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the shop's events most likely helped it by","choices":["attracting the chain store's employees.","replacing book sales entirely.","giving customers reasons to visit that the chain store did not offer.","lowering the prices of all its books."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Event-related sales rose while the shop built a community. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e52bde9f31","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-79","passage":"<p>Some bird species are known to use the sun and stars to navigate, but a study of young songbirds raised indoors suggests that they may also inherit a sense of direction. Birds that had never migrated and had never seen the sky still became restless and tried to fly in the correct migratory direction at the right time of year. The researchers propose that young birds may be born with a rough compass that experience later refines.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"that experience later refines\" suggests that","choices":["birds lose their sense of direction as they age.","birds' navigation likely becomes more accurate as they gain experience migrating.","birds raised indoors can never migrate successfully.","experience is unnecessary for navigation."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An inborn rough compass becomes more precise with experience. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.019ddb8333","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-80","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My first job was washing dishes in a busy diner. I thought of it only as a way to earn money for a bicycle. But the cook, a woman named Dolores, noticed me watching her flip eggs and began teaching me during slow hours: how to crack an egg with one hand, how to tell by sound when the oil was hot. Thirty years and two restaurants later, I still crack eggs the way she taught me.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Dolores began teaching the narrator most likely because she","choices":["was asked to by the diner's owner.","needed someone to replace her immediately.","wanted the narrator to stop washing dishes.","noticed the narrator's interest in cooking."],"answer":"D","explanation":"She \"noticed me watching her flip eggs.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c81fd64baa","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-81","passage":"<p>Roads built through forests can divide wildlife habitats, making it dangerous for animals to cross. In one mountain region, engineers built bridges covered with soil and native plants over a busy highway. Motion cameras showed deer, bears, and even small mammals using the crossings regularly. Collisions between vehicles and large animals on that stretch of highway dropped by more than eighty percent.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the bridges were covered with soil and native plants most likely to","choices":["prevent animals from using them.","reduce the cost of building them.","make the bridges easier for cars to use.","make the crossings resemble the animals' natural habitat."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Natural cover would encourage wildlife to cross. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ec9d50fbc1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-84","passage":"<p>Many schools ban phones in classrooms, arguing that the devices distract students. One district tested a policy requiring students to store phones in lockers during the school day. Over a year, teachers reported fewer interruptions and students' test scores rose slightly, with the largest gains among students who had previously struggled. Some students, however, reported feeling anxious without access to their phones, especially in the first weeks.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the policy's benefits for test scores appeared to be","choices":["limited to students who already had high scores.","greatest for students who had been struggling.","entirely absent.","largest in the first few weeks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The largest gains were \"among students who had previously struggled.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f498eae98f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-85","passage":"<p>Paper was invented in China and spread gradually westward over many centuries. For much of that time, knowledge of papermaking was closely guarded. Historians note that papermaking reached new regions most often through trade routes and the movement of skilled workers, who brought their techniques with them. Once established, local paper mills made written records cheaper and far more common.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a region that received skilled papermakers along a trade route would most likely","choices":["have no need for trade.","stop keeping written records.","soon begin producing paper locally.","keep papermaking techniques completely secret."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Techniques spread with the workers, leading to local mills. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.00b72bc50c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-86","passage":"<p>Mangrove trees grow in salty coastal water, where most plants cannot survive. Some mangroves filter salt out at their roots, while others absorb salt and then push it out through their leaves, which can be seen coated with white crystals. Mangrove forests also protect shorelines by slowing waves with their dense, tangled roots.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, white crystals on a mangrove's leaves are most likely","choices":["salt that the tree has pushed out.","sand blown from the beach.","a sign that the tree is diseased.","frost from cold weather."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some mangroves \"push it out through their leaves.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.80be94998f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-88","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The storm had passed, but the town was silent. No cars, no radios, no hum of refrigerators. Luis walked outside and heard things he had never noticed: water dripping from the gutter in a steady rhythm, a dog barking three streets away, his neighbor laughing on her porch. He stood there a long time, listening to a town he thought he knew.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"a town he thought he knew\" suggests that Luis","choices":["plans to leave the town soon.","realizes there is more to his familiar town than he had noticed.","does not recognize his neighbor.","has just moved to the town."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He discovers unnoticed sounds in a familiar place. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.da3d5f2fd3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-92","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a letter written by a young teacher in the early twentieth century.</p><p>The schoolhouse has one room, one stove, and twenty-three children between the ages of six and fifteen. I teach all of them at once. The older ones help the younger with their letters, and I have come to believe they learn as much by teaching as by listening to me. Yesterday a boy of twelve explained long division to a girl of eight more clearly than I could have.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the teacher's attitude toward the older students' help is best described as","choices":["appreciative.","resentful.","indifferent.","suspicious."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She believes they learn from it and praises the boy's explanation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f13cdc793f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-96","passage":"<p>When a remote island installed solar panels and batteries to replace its diesel generators, officials worried that cloudy weeks would leave residents without power. The system's designers addressed the concern by oversizing the battery bank and keeping one small generator for emergencies. In the first three years, the generator ran for a total of only eleven days, and the island's fuel costs fell by more than ninety percent.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the generator ran for only eleven days most likely because","choices":["the generator was broken.","residents stopped using electricity.","the island had no cloudy weather for three years.","the batteries usually stored enough solar energy to cover cloudy periods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The oversized battery bank reduced the need for backup power. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e9dfe4a7d2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-97","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Aunt Rosa's kitchen radio had been broken for years, stuck forever on a station that played old dance music. Everyone offered to buy her a new one. She refused. When I asked her why, she turned up the volume, took my hands, and spun me across the linoleum floor, laughing, until the song ended. \"Now,\" she said, slightly out of breath, \"you tell me what's broken.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Aunt Rosa refused a new radio most likely because","choices":["new radios were too expensive.","she rarely spent time in the kitchen.","she wanted to learn to repair it herself.","she enjoyed the music the old radio played."],"answer":"D","explanation":"She dances happily to the station it is stuck on. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fe91effb92","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-98","passage":"<p>Ecologists studying a river noticed that trout populations were healthiest in stretches shaded by trees. Shade keeps water cooler, and cooler water holds more dissolved oxygen, which trout need. Where trees along the banks had been cleared for pasture, water temperatures rose in summer and trout numbers fell. Several landowners have since planted trees along the river to restore shade.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a stretch of river where trees are planted would most likely","choices":["require more pasture.","become too cold for trout to survive.","eventually become more suitable for trout.","lose dissolved oxygen."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shade restores cooler, oxygen-rich water. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a01c5331eb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-99","passage":"<p>In the 1800s, many doctors did not wash their hands between examining patients. One physician noticed that women giving birth in a hospital ward staffed by doctors died of fever far more often than women in a ward staffed by midwives, who did not perform autopsies. He suspected that doctors were carrying something harmful from autopsies to patients and required them to wash their hands with a chlorine solution. Death rates in the doctors' ward fell dramatically.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the fact that midwives did not perform autopsies was significant because it","choices":["meant midwives worked fewer hours than doctors.","helped explain why their patients were less likely to develop fever.","proved that midwives were better trained.","caused the physician to ban midwives."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The key difference between the wards was autopsy work. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.465bd43535","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-102","passage":"<p>Many people believe that bulls are enraged by the color red. In fact, cattle cannot distinguish red from green. Experiments have shown that bulls charge at a moving cape regardless of its color, suggesting that the motion, not the color, provokes them. The red cape's use in bullfighting likely has more to do with tradition and showmanship than with any effect on the animal.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a bull facing a still red cape and a moving blue cape would most likely","choices":["ignore both capes.","charge only at a green cape.","charge at the still red cape.","charge at the moving blue cape."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Motion provokes them regardless of color. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.91f148174a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-105","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The chess club had lost every match that season. At the final tournament, twelve-year-old Imani faced the league's top player, a high school senior who had not lost a game in two years. Forty minutes in, the senior frowned, studied the board for a long time, and then extended his hand across the table. The room went silent. Imani stared at his hand before she understood.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Imani \"stared at his hand before she understood\" most likely because she","choices":["thought he was asking for a pencil.","did not immediately realize that he was conceding the game.","did not want to shake hands with him.","was unsure whether the tournament had begun."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An offered handshake signals resignation, which she had not expected. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a1c56d37aa","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-106","passage":"<p>When a river city experienced repeated floods, planners considered building taller levees. Instead, they converted a strip of low-lying land along the river into a park designed to flood safely, with grassy fields and paths that could be submerged without serious damage. During heavy rains, the park absorbs overflow that would otherwise reach nearby streets. In dry months, residents use it for sports and picnics.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the park's grassy fields and paths were most likely chosen because they","choices":["can be flooded without being seriously damaged.","prevent all water from reaching the park.","are rarely used by residents.","cost more than other materials."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The park was designed to be submerged safely. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.41f626df65","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-108","passage":"<p>Honeybee colonies are sometimes described as \"superorganisms\" because individual bees function somewhat like cells in a body. Worker bees specialize in tasks such as foraging, nursing larvae, and guarding the entrance, and the colony as a whole regulates its own temperature. No single bee directs these activities; instead, they emerge from countless interactions among individuals responding to simple cues.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the comparison to \"cells in a body\" suggests that individual bees","choices":["contribute to the colony's survival through specialized roles.","compete with one another for control.","can survive independently for long periods.","all perform identical jobs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Like cells, bees specialize for the whole. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.acf4b1b35c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-110","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Wen had moved to the city expecting noise, crowds, and strangers who never looked at one another. What surprised her was the bakery on the corner. The owner learned her name on her second visit, set aside a sesame roll for her each morning, and asked about her new job as though they had known each other for years. By winter, the corner no longer felt like part of a strange city. It felt like her street.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the owner setting aside a sesame roll each morning most likely shows that the owner","choices":["thought Wen was a new employee.","wanted Wen to buy more bread.","did not have enough rolls for other customers.","paid attention to Wen and made her feel welcome."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It is one of several personal gestures that make Wen feel known. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.81d9ae9b28","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-111","passage":"<p>In the early years of aviation, many engineers tried to build flying machines that flapped their wings like birds. These designs rarely worked. Later inventors achieved sustained flight by separating two functions that birds' wings combine: fixed wings provided lift, while propellers provided forward thrust. Studying birds was useful, but copying them too literally had led engineers astray.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"copying them too literally\" most likely refers to","choices":["studying how birds build nests.","using feathers as building materials.","painting airplanes to look like birds.","attempting to recreate birds' flapping motion in machines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Flapping designs \"rarely worked.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9bb87221b4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-117","passage":"<p>Paleontologists once imagined large dinosaurs as slow, cold-blooded animals that dragged their tails. Evidence from fossil trackways, which show tails held off the ground, and from bone growth rings, which suggest rapid growth, has changed that view. Many scientists now picture dinosaurs as active animals, some possibly warm-blooded, with tails held out for balance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, fossil trackways without tail marks are significant because they suggest that dinosaurs","choices":["had no tails.","walked only in water.","held their tails off the ground.","dragged their tails constantly."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Trackways \"show tails held off the ground.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.56eb23751e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-118","passage":"<p>A city parks department noticed that its public benches were rarely used. Designers observed that most benches faced empty lawns or walls. They moved several benches to face walkways, playgrounds, and a fountain, where people could watch activity. Within weeks, the relocated benches were among the most used in the park, while those left in place remained mostly empty.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the designers would most likely conclude that people choose where to sit partly based on","choices":["how far the benches are from the parking lot.","how new the benches are.","whether the benches are made of wood.","whether there is something interesting to watch."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benches facing activity became the most used. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ae5688856d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-120","passage":"<p>Beekeepers in one region reported that their honey tasted different from year to year. Chemists analyzing samples found that the flavor compounds in the honey closely matched the flowers that had bloomed most heavily each season. In years when wild thyme was abundant, for example, the honey contained compounds associated with thyme's distinctive flavor.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, honey produced in a year when lavender bloomed heavily would most likely","choices":["have no flavor at all.","contain flavor compounds associated with lavender.","contain compounds associated with thyme only.","taste exactly like the previous year's honey."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Honey flavor reflects the most abundant flowers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2cd962755e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-122","passage":"<p>Coastal fog is an important water source for some redwood forests. Scientists measuring moisture found that during dry summers, redwoods absorb water from fog through their leaves, and fog dripping from branches can supply a substantial share of the water reaching the forest floor. Researchers are concerned that warming ocean temperatures could reduce fog along the coast.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a summer with much less coastal fog would most likely","choices":["have no effect on redwood forests.","cause redwoods to absorb more water through their roots from fog.","make redwood forests wetter than usual.","leave redwood forests with less water during the dry season."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fog provides water \"during dry summers.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.b23107133d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-123","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The science fair judges had moved on, and Dario's volcano had not erupted. He had checked the vinegar and the baking soda twice. His friends drifted away to watch a robot that played music. Alone at his table, Dario lifted the lid of the volcano and saw it: a folded note from his little sister, taped inside, blocking the tube. It read, \"Good luck!!!\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the note in the volcano is best described as","choices":["a deliberate attempt to ruin the project.","a kind gesture that accidentally caused a problem.","instructions for fixing the volcano.","a message from the judges."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It says \"Good luck!!!\" yet blocked the tube. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a351f46bf2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-124","passage":"<p>When a hospital added a small garden where patients could sit outdoors, staff expected it to be used mainly by visitors. Instead, nurses began bringing patients recovering from surgery outside for short periods. Records later showed that these patients requested less pain medication on average than similar patients who stayed indoors, though the researchers noted that the patients who went outside may have been healthier to begin with.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely agree that","choices":["pain medication should be eliminated.","the garden should be closed.","patients who stayed indoors were healthier.","a study that randomly assigned patients to go outdoors would give stronger evidence."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Their caution suggests a need to rule out preexisting differences. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.caa055ed6d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-125","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather had kept bees for fifty years, and he spoke to them every morning. \"Good morning, ladies,\" he would say, lifting the lid. My mother said it was nonsense. But the summer he was in the hospital and she tended the hives, I heard her through the kitchen window, standing by the boxes in her bathrobe, saying softly, \"Good morning, ladies. He'll be home soon.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the mother's words to the bees most likely reveal","choices":["her plan to sell the hives.","her belief that the bees were ill.","her annoyance at having to wake up early.","her concern for her father and her wish to keep his routine going."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"He'll be home soon\" shows care and continuity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8a3eb18108","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-127","passage":"<p>In the 1970s, a city tore down an elevated highway that divided its waterfront from downtown. Many business owners predicted traffic chaos. Traffic did shift to other streets, but congestion did not increase as much as feared, partly because some drivers changed their routes or schedules and some switched to transit. The land along the water became a popular promenade lined with restaurants.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, congestion increased less than predicted mainly because","choices":["the population of the city fell.","the city banned cars downtown.","drivers adapted by changing routes, schedules, or modes of travel.","a new highway was built elsewhere."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Some drivers changed routes or schedules and some switched to transit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.971dcc07f2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-129","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>We tend to think of maps as neutral pictures of the world, but every map involves choices. A mapmaker decides what to include and what to leave out, which places deserve large labels, and what to put at the center. A subway map that shows stations evenly spaced, for instance, is useful for riders precisely because it distorts real distances.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the writer would most likely agree that a map's value depends partly on","choices":["how old it is.","how many features it includes.","whether it shows exact distances.","how well its choices fit the needs of its users."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subway map is useful because of its distortion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f4c8186b02","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-131","passage":"<p>Many species of songbird learn their songs by listening to adults during a sensitive period early in life. Researchers raising young birds in soundproof rooms found that birds that never heard adult songs developed simple, unusual songs. Birds that heard recordings of their own species during the sensitive period developed normal songs, while those exposed only after the period ended did not.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a young songbird that first hears adult songs after its sensitive period would most likely","choices":["fail to develop a fully normal song.","develop a normal song immediately.","learn songs of other species instead.","stop singing altogether."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Birds exposed after the period \"did not\" develop normal songs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.635a205f5c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-132","passage":"<p>Some libraries have eliminated late fees. Supporters argue that fees discourage low-income patrons, who may stop borrowing books rather than risk charges. One library system that removed fees found that many cardholders who had been blocked because of unpaid fines returned, and the rate at which books came back on time barely changed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a concern about eliminating late fees that the library system's results address is that","choices":["patrons would stop returning books on time.","fewer people would visit libraries.","libraries would lose all their books.","staff would need more training."],"answer":"A","explanation":"On-time return rates \"barely changed.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ef981d3d57","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-134","passage":"<p>Researchers studying ants noticed that when a colony's usual path to food was blocked, the ants quickly found a new route. Individual ants wandered somewhat randomly, but those that found food laid down chemical trails on the way back. Shorter routes were traveled more often, so their trails grew stronger, attracting more ants. In time, most ants followed the shortest available path, even though no single ant planned it.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, shorter routes develop stronger trails because","choices":["longer routes have no chemical trails.","ants complete trips on them more often, adding chemicals more frequently.","ants avoid walking on longer routes entirely.","they are chosen by the colony's queen."],"answer":"B","explanation":"More frequent travel reinforces the trail. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fc42cbcf30","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-136","passage":"<p>A company that manufactures outdoor jackets began offering free repairs for life. Executives worried that the program would reduce sales, since customers would buy fewer new jackets. Instead, sales rose. Surveys suggested that customers valued the promise that their jackets would last, and many chose the brand specifically because of the repair program.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, many customers most likely viewed the repair program as","choices":["a sign that the jackets were poorly made.","evidence that the jackets were a worthwhile long-term purchase.","an unnecessary expense.","a reason to buy jackets from a competitor."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Customers \"valued the promise that their jackets would last.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.7b4596d94b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-139","passage":"<p>For much of the twentieth century, doctors advised patients with back pain to rest in bed. Later studies found that patients who stayed active, within limits, often recovered faster than those who rested. Many guidelines now recommend gentle movement, such as walking, for most cases of short-term back pain.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a patient with short-term back pain following current guidelines would most likely be advised to","choices":["take short, gentle walks.","lift heavy weights immediately.","avoid all movement.","stay in bed for several weeks."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Guidelines \"recommend gentle movement, such as walking.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.23ab8203ea","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-140","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient village, they found that one house contained far more imported pottery, jewelry, and fine tools than the others. Some suggested that a wealthy chief lived there. Others noted that the house was also unusually large and contained many storage pits, and proposed that it may have been a communal building where valuable goods were kept for the whole village.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which finding would most support the idea that the building was communal?","choices":["Evidence that goods in the storage pits were distributed to many households","Decorations showing one person's name","A single bed in the house","A burial of a single wealthy individual beneath the house"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Distribution to many households fits communal storage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f23ca03fe2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-141","passage":"<p>Some city governments have begun painting large murals on the walls of buildings in neighborhoods with high rates of graffiti. In several cities, murals were rarely tagged with graffiti, while plain walls nearby continued to be marked. Observers suggest that graffiti writers may respect the murals as art, or that the busy designs leave little blank space that invites tagging.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a large, plain wall in the same neighborhood would most likely","choices":["be chosen for a mural by graffiti writers.","be more likely to receive graffiti than a wall with a mural.","be tagged less often than a mural.","never be tagged with graffiti."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Plain walls nearby continued to be marked.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4a865cf7ae","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-142","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father repaired watches in a narrow shop with a single window. As a child, I thought his work was boring: hours bent over tiny gears, not speaking. Only when I tried it myself, years later, did I understand. The silence was not emptiness. It was concentration so complete that the rest of the world simply stepped aside.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the narrator's understanding of her father's work changed because she","choices":["read a book about watchmaking.","experienced the concentration it required firsthand.","visited a larger watch factory.","asked her father to explain his silence."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Only when I tried it myself.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1e745cb57e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-143","passage":"<p>Many people assume that pandas eat bamboo because it is highly nutritious. In fact, bamboo is low in nutrients, and pandas digest only a small portion of what they eat. To survive, they eat for up to fourteen hours a day and conserve energy by moving relatively little. Their low-energy lifestyle is an adaptation to a diet that provides little fuel.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if pandas became much more active, they would most likely","choices":["digest bamboo more efficiently.","stop eating bamboo.","be able to eat less bamboo.","need to find even more food to meet their energy needs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Their low activity compensates for limited energy from food. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.913270699b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-144","passage":"<p>A study of online product reviews found that reviews rated as \"helpful\" by other shoppers tended to mention both positive and negative aspects of a product. Entirely positive reviews were rated helpful less often, perhaps because shoppers suspected them of being exaggerated or written by the seller.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a seller hoping to write a product description that shoppers trust might","choices":["acknowledge some limitations of the product.","describe the product only in glowing terms.","avoid mentioning the product's features.","remove all reviews from the website."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Balanced information appears more credible. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3ab7528b78","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-145","passage":"<p>When a coastal town was hit by a severe storm, residents who lived near a restored salt marsh reported less flooding than those living near a stretch of shoreline protected only by a concrete wall. Scientists noted that the marsh's plants slowed incoming waves and absorbed water, and that, unlike the wall, the marsh had not been damaged by the storm.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which statement about the concrete wall is best supported?","choices":["It was damaged by the storm.","It protected residents from all flooding.","It slowed waves more effectively than plants.","It absorbed more water than the marsh."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Unlike the wall, the marsh had not been damaged.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ee7fe56917","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-148","passage":"<p>A researcher studying how people choose where to sit in cafés found that most customers first selected seats along walls or near windows, leaving tables in the middle of the room empty until the café was crowded. The researcher suggests that people may prefer seats that offer a view while protecting their backs, a preference that may reflect a long-standing wariness of open spaces.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a café owner who wanted center tables to be used more often might","choices":["place center tables closer to the door.","make center tables smaller.","remove all windows.","add partitions or plants that make those tables feel more sheltered."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sheltering center seats might satisfy the protective preference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0b9bc0db5d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-149","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars believed that a certain medieval poem was the work of a single author. A computer analysis of word choices and sentence patterns found distinct differences between the first half of the poem and the second. Some scholars now suggest that a second poet completed the work, though others argue that a single author's style may simply have changed over many years of writing.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the scholars who favor a single author would most likely point to evidence that","choices":["the two halves are written in different languages.","the poem was written over a long period.","the poem was written in a single week.","two different names appear in the manuscript."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A style may change \"over many years of writing.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.18a2866b53","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-151","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every photograph in the hallway showed Grandmother smiling, but Lena had never once seen her smile in person. At the funeral, an old woman Lena did not recognize took her hand and said, \"Your grandmother laughed louder than anyone I ever knew, before the war.\" Lena looked back at the hallway photographs and noticed, for the first time, that all of them had been taken long ago.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"before the war\" most likely suggests that","choices":["the photographs were taken during the war.","the old woman met the grandmother after the war.","the grandmother's experiences during the war changed her.","the grandmother did not live through the war."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Her laughter belongs to the time before the war. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.32f1fb07a9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-157","passage":"<p>Ancient Polynesian navigators crossed vast stretches of the Pacific without instruments. They read the positions of stars, the direction and shape of ocean swells, and the flight paths of seabirds, which head out to sea in the morning and return to land at dusk. By combining these clues, navigators could locate islands far beyond the horizon.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a navigator who saw seabirds flying in a certain direction at dusk would most likely conclude that","choices":["a storm was approaching.","the birds were heading out to sea.","land lay in that direction.","the canoe was sailing away from land."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Seabirds \"return to land at dusk.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fd078e112f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-158","passage":"<p>Researchers found that people shopping online tend to buy more when a website shows how many other people have recently purchased an item. When the counter was removed from one site, sales of those items fell modestly. The researchers suggest that people use others' choices as a shortcut when they are uncertain about what to buy.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, purchase counters would most likely have the greatest effect when shoppers","choices":["are unsure which product to choose.","are buying items they have purchased before.","shop in physical stores.","already know exactly what they want."],"answer":"A","explanation":"People use others' choices \"when they are uncertain.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.06520b5961","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-161","passage":"<p>Early astronomers noticed that the planet Mars sometimes appeared to move backward across the sky for several weeks before resuming its usual direction. Some explained this with complex systems of circles within circles. The motion is now understood as an effect of perspective: Earth, moving faster in its orbit, periodically overtakes Mars, making Mars appear to slip backward.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a similar backward motion might be observed for another planet if","choices":["Earth passed it while both orbited the Sun.","the planet were closer to Earth than the Moon.","Earth moved more slowly than the planet.","the planet stopped orbiting."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The effect results from Earth overtaking a planet. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0c8da2b1ef","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-163","passage":"<p>Salmon that hatch in rivers spend years in the ocean before returning to spawn. When they die after spawning, their bodies decompose along riverbanks. Scientists have found traces of ocean nitrogen in streamside trees, delivered by salmon carcasses dragged into the forest by bears. The fish, in effect, carry nutrients from the sea into the forest.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a decline in salmon returning to a river would most likely","choices":["reduce the marine nutrients reaching nearby forests.","increase nitrogen in streamside trees.","cause bears to plant more trees.","have no effect on the forest."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Fewer carcasses means fewer delivered nutrients. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a3809915c6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-164","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandmother never threw anything away. Jars held buttons, rubber bands, and bits of string. As a teenager I found this embarrassing. Then, during a week-long power outage, our neighbors came to her door one by one, asking for candles, batteries, a length of wire, a spare key. She had everything. She handed it all over with a small, satisfied smile.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grandmother's \"small, satisfied smile\" most likely suggests that she","choices":["was embarrassed by her collection.","felt her habit had been proven worthwhile.","was annoyed by the neighbors' requests.","wanted the neighbors to pay her."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Her saving habit was vindicated. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.445a96f53e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-166","passage":"<p>Linguists recording conversations noticed that speakers often pause briefly before important or unexpected words. Listeners, in experiments, were better at remembering words that followed such pauses. The researchers suggest that pauses may help listeners by signaling that something worth attention is coming.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a teacher who wanted students to remember a key term might","choices":["say it as quickly as possible.","pause briefly before saying it.","say it only in writing.","avoid pausing during the lesson."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Pauses improved memory for following words. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0538f40ffc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-170","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The old bridge knows the river's moods: / the spring flood's rushing rage, / the summer's lazy silver hum, / the winter's frozen stage. / It does not fight the water, / it simply holds its ground, / and lets each season pass beneath / without a single sound.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the bridge is presented as","choices":["patient and resilient.","constantly changing.","fragile and fearful.","in conflict with the river."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It \"does not fight\" yet \"holds its ground.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4786878f49","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-171","passage":"<p>Researchers studying how people read food labels used eye-tracking devices and found that most shoppers looked at the calorie count and the price but rarely read the ingredient list. When the ingredient list was moved to the front of the package and printed in larger type, shoppers were far more likely to read it.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the placement and size of information on packages most likely","choices":["have no effect on shoppers.","influence whether shoppers notice it.","make shoppers less likely to buy food.","matter only for prices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Moving and enlarging the list increased reading. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3dd769f9a6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-178","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The old clock in the hallway had stopped at 4:17 the day Grandfather died, and no one had wound it since. Years passed. Then, on the morning of her own wedding, Tessa found her father standing in front of it with the key in his hand. He wound it slowly, and as the pendulum began to swing, he said, without turning around, \"It's time.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the father's words \"It's time\" most likely have","choices":["an angry tone toward Tessa.","both a literal meaning about the clock and a figurative meaning about moving on.","no connection to the clock.","only a meaning about being late for the wedding."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The restarted clock and the wedding suggest a new chapter. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3c399025c2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-182","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>As a child I believed that adults knew exactly what they were doing. My parents paid bills, fixed leaks, and made decisions with what looked like total confidence. It was only when I became an adult myself that I realized how much of that confidence was performance, a steady face kept up so that children would feel safe while the grown-ups figured things out.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the writer suggests that her parents appeared confident mainly to","choices":["hide their wealth.","avoid doing household tasks.","help their children feel secure.","impress their neighbors."],"answer":"C","explanation":"So that \"children would feel safe.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.cb239207d6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-184","passage":"<p>A company that produces breakfast cereal tested whether placing a cartoon character on the box affected children's choices. In a taste test, children were given the same cereal in two boxes, one with a character and one without. Most children said the cereal from the character box tasted better, even though the cereal was identical.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the test suggests that children's judgments of taste can be","choices":["influenced by factors unrelated to the food itself.","identical for all foods.","completely unaffected by packaging.","based only on nutritional information."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Packaging changed taste ratings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8b6f86ebc6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-186","passage":"<p>A school district that shortened its summer break and added more short breaks throughout the year hoped to reduce learning loss. Teachers reported spending less time reviewing material in the fall. Some families, however, struggled to find child care during the new breaks, which did not align with the schedules of nearby districts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the child care difficulties were made worse by the fact that","choices":["teachers worked during the breaks.","the district had no schools.","other districts' breaks occurred at different times.","the summer break was longer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Breaks \"did not align with the schedules of nearby districts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.179dac9f58","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-194","passage":"<p>Engineers testing self-driving cars found that the vehicles handled highway driving well but struggled in situations requiring interpretation of human behavior, such as a pedestrian waving a driver forward or a cyclist making eye contact before turning. Human drivers rely on such informal signals, which are difficult to program.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a self-driving car would most likely have the greatest difficulty","choices":["driving in a straight line.","at a crowded crosswalk where pedestrians gesture to drivers.","following marked lanes.","on an empty highway in clear weather."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Human gestures are hard for the vehicles. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8f19000308","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-195","passage":"<p>Some islands have species of birds that cannot fly, even though their ancestors could. Biologists suggest that on islands without ground predators, flying offered few advantages while requiring a great deal of energy. Over many generations, birds that spent less energy on flight muscles and more on other traits may have survived and reproduced more successfully.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if predators that hunt on the ground were introduced to such an island, the flightless birds would most likely","choices":["be unaffected.","be at greater risk than birds that can fly.","begin hunting the predators.","quickly regain the ability to fly."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They evolved in the absence of ground predators. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.edf0fcc5f7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-196","passage":"<p>When a library replaced its self-checkout machines with staffed desks for a trial period, checkout times increased slightly. Surveys, however, showed that patrons asked librarians many more questions, often about finding books, using databases, or getting help with job applications. Several patrons said they had not realized librarians could help with such tasks.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, patrons who used self-checkout machines had probably","choices":["checked out fewer books.","been less aware of the help librarians could offer.","asked librarians more questions.","waited in longer lines."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Some \"had not realized librarians could help.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.39f62a337e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-197","passage":"<p>When a coastal city's fishing industry declined, many old wooden boats were left to rot in the harbor. A group of retired shipwrights began restoring a few of them and offering free sailing lessons to local teenagers. The project grew into a program that now teaches boatbuilding skills, and several graduates have found jobs at repair yards along the coast.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the program most likely benefits the community by","choices":["ending sailing lessons.","preserving traditional skills and creating job opportunities for young people.","increasing the number of fishing boats that rot.","replacing the city's harbor."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Graduates work at repair yards. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.05830f037e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-199","passage":"<p>Researchers who installed underwater microphones near a restored oyster reef found that the reef grew noisier over several years, filled with the clicks and grunts of fish and shrimp. The noise levels closely tracked increases in the numbers of animals divers counted. The researchers suggest that recording sound could offer a cheaper way to monitor reef recovery than frequent diving surveys.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a restored reef whose recordings stayed quiet for several years would most likely","choices":["have too many fish for divers to count.","be recovering faster than other reefs.","not be attracting many animals.","need fewer microphones."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sound tracked animal abundance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a73f588f1a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-205","passage":"<p>Astronomers studying a distant star noticed that its brightness dipped irregularly and by unusually large amounts, unlike the small, regular dips caused by orbiting planets. Some proposed that swarms of comets or clouds of dust were passing in front of it. Later observations showing that the dimming was stronger in blue light than red light supported the dust explanation, since fine dust blocks blue light more.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if the star's dimming had been equal in blue and red light, the dust explanation would most likely have been","choices":["proven correct.","replaced by the planet explanation automatically.","less strongly supported.","unaffected."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Dust predicts stronger blue dimming. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4fce8bb9bd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-206","passage":"<p>When an airline began allowing passengers to board from both the front and back doors of the plane, the average boarding time dropped by several minutes. Crowding in the aisle decreased because passengers seated near the rear no longer had to squeeze past those stowing bags in front rows.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, passengers seated in the rear were most likely delayed before the change because they","choices":["boarded before everyone else.","used a different airplane.","had to wait while people in front rows put away their bags.","had no overhead bins."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They had to \"squeeze past those stowing bags.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d588319f40","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-207","passage":"<p>Some trees in dense forests grow tall and thin, while trees of the same species growing in open fields spread wide branches and remain shorter. In a crowded forest, sunlight is limited at lower levels, so growing upward quickly helps a tree reach light. In an open field, sunlight is available all around, and wide branches can capture it efficiently.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a young tree growing in a gap where a large tree has fallen in the forest would most likely","choices":["stop growing entirely.","receive less light than before the tree fell.","have more light available at lower levels than trees in dense forest.","grow only underground."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A gap opens the canopy. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.400c6691dc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-208","passage":"<p>When a school replaced traditional detention with a program in which students discussed what led to their behavior and how to make amends, repeat offenses declined. Teachers noted that students were more likely to apologize and less likely to see discipline as unfair. Some teachers, however, felt the conversations took too much time during busy school days.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, students in the program were more likely to view discipline as fair most likely because","choices":["they had a chance to discuss their behavior and its effects.","teachers stopped enforcing rules.","they received no consequences.","detention lasted longer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Discussion made discipline feel more reasonable. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4b21898bfe","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-209","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, Grandpa Luis entered the county fair's pie contest, and every summer he lost to Mrs. Delgado. This year, when the judges announced that he had finally won, he did not cheer. He walked straight to Mrs. Delgado's table, cut a slice of her peach pie, and said, loudly enough for everyone to hear, \"Still the best in the county.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Grandpa Luis most likely speaks \"loudly enough for everyone to hear\" in order to","choices":["get a second prize.","embarrass Mrs. Delgado.","publicly honor Mrs. Delgado's skill.","complain about the judges."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A public compliment shows respect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f6c28927b6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-213","passage":"<p>When a bakery began selling day-old bread at half price, the owner expected sales of fresh bread to fall. Instead, fresh bread sales held steady, and the discounted bread attracted new customers, some of whom later bought fresh items. Food waste at the bakery also dropped.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, some new customers who bought discounted bread most likely","choices":["bought only fresh bread from the start.","stopped visiting the bakery.","complained about the prices.","returned to buy fresh items as well."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Some of whom later bought fresh items.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.699b7fcbc2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-215","passage":"<p>A city testing electric buses found that they cost more to buy than diesel buses but less to fuel and maintain. Over a bus's expected lifetime, the total costs were roughly equal. The electric buses also reduced noise and exhaust along routes through dense neighborhoods, which residents reported appreciating.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the city would most likely consider electric buses worthwhile because","choices":["they required more fuel.","residents preferred louder buses.","their total cost was similar while offering environmental and quality-of-life benefits.","they were much cheaper to purchase."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Equal costs plus less noise and exhaust. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.20038483de","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-216","passage":"<p>Many hikers assume that a trail's difficulty depends mainly on its length. A park service that surveyed visitors found that elevation gain and uneven footing predicted how tiring people found a trail far better than distance did. Several short, steep, rocky trails were rated harder than much longer trails over gentle ground. The park revised its trail signs to list elevation gain alongside distance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the revised signs were most likely intended to","choices":["help visitors judge a trail's difficulty more accurately.","shorten the trails.","discourage visitors from hiking.","replace maps entirely."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Elevation gain predicted tiredness better. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c00c76a77b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-217","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>For years, Ines had walked past the abandoned greenhouse on her way to work, noting its broken panes and the vines pressing against the glass. One morning she stopped, pushed open the rusted door, and stepped inside. Among the weeds, a single orange tree still stood, heavy with small, bright fruit, as if it had been waiting all this time for someone to notice.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"as if it had been waiting all this time for someone to notice\" suggests that Ines","choices":["planted the tree years ago.","expected the tree to be there.","is disappointed by what she finds.","feels a sense of wonder at finding the tree."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The image conveys surprise and wonder. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a8fe8cbdd5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-219","passage":"<p>When a city first installed bike lanes painted on the road, many cyclists still felt unsafe riding next to fast traffic. The city later tested lanes separated from cars by concrete barriers. On streets with protected lanes, the number of cyclists rose sharply, especially among people who described themselves as cautious riders.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, protected lanes most likely attracted cautious riders because they","choices":["were painted brighter colors.","made cyclists feel safer from nearby traffic.","allowed cars to drive in them.","were located only in parks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Riders \"felt unsafe riding next to fast traffic\" otherwise. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.871a378360","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-220","passage":"<p>Some caves contain formations called stalactites, which hang from ceilings like icicles. They form over thousands of years as water dripping from the ceiling leaves behind tiny amounts of dissolved minerals. Because they grow so slowly, scientists can analyze their layers to learn about past rainfall and climate in the region.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a period of very little rainfall would most likely be reflected in a stalactite as","choices":["no change at all.","a sudden increase in its length.","the stalactite falling from the ceiling.","a change in its layers, since less water would drip from the ceiling."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Layers record water and mineral deposition. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4d12ae9d65","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-221","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandfather taught me to fish on a lake so quiet that you could hear a dragonfly land. He never seemed to care whether we caught anything. When I asked him why we kept coming back on days when the fish would not bite, he pointed his rod at the mist rising off the water and said, \"We're not really here for the fish.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grandfather's gesture toward the mist suggests that he","choices":["was ready to leave.","thought the mist scared the fish.","appreciated the beauty of the lake.","wanted to fish in another spot."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He points to the scenery while explaining why they come. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.38fef2c1a1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-222","passage":"<p>When an online retailer began showing delivery dates rather than shipping speeds, such as \"arrives Thursday\" instead of \"ships in three to five days,\" customers were more likely to complete purchases. Researchers suggest that a specific date is easier for shoppers to understand and plan around than a range of days.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a shopper needing a gift by a certain day would most likely find which information most useful?","choices":["A general shipping speed range","The location of the warehouse","The weight of the package","A specific expected arrival date"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Specific dates help planning. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.dc28ec2388","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-223","passage":"<p>Scientists studying a mountain lake found that its water had become clearer over several decades. At first they suspected reduced pollution. Further investigation showed that an invasive species of mussel, which filters tiny particles from the water, had spread throughout the lake. The mussels made the water clearer but also removed plankton that native fish depend on for food.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, native fish populations in the lake would most likely","choices":["begin eating the mussels exclusively.","decline because of reduced food.","remain unaffected by the mussels.","increase because of clearer water."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Mussels removed plankton the fish depend on. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d9832a9514","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-224","passage":"<p>Some schools have introduced \"reading buddies\" programs in which older students read with younger ones. Teachers in one school reported that younger children's reading improved, as expected. They were surprised to find that many older students who struggled with reading also showed gains, perhaps because reading aloud to younger children gave them low-pressure practice.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, reading aloud to younger children most likely helped struggling older students because it","choices":["required them to read difficult novels.","involved competitive tests.","let them practice in a comfortable, low-pressure setting.","replaced their regular classes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Low-pressure practice.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3b65fc93cd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-225","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The lamp in the window of the corner store / has burned each night for forty years. / Travelers who have never met the owner / measure their journeys by its light, / and though it saves no lives, / it tells them, turning the last corner, / that someone, somewhere on this street, / is still awake.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, travelers \"measure their journeys by its light\" most likely because the lamp","choices":["serves as a familiar landmark signaling they are near their destination.","belongs to their family.","is the only light in the city.","shows the exact distance they have traveled."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It marks \"turning the last corner.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fbb8f55d6a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-227","passage":"<p>A study of restaurant tipping found that servers who wrote \"thank you\" on customers' bills received slightly higher tips on average than servers who did not. The effect was small but consistent across many meals. The researchers suggest that small gestures of friendliness can influence how people respond, even in routine transactions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely agree that","choices":["tips are determined entirely by food quality.","servers should stop thanking customers.","customers dislike messages on bills.","minor friendly gestures can make a measurable difference in people's behavior."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Small gestures \"can influence how people respond.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.44140fbd3d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-229","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The night before the move, Sara walked through the empty house one last time. Without furniture, every room sounded different, her footsteps echoing where the couch and bookshelves had once absorbed them. In her old bedroom, she pressed her hand against the wall where she had marked her height each birthday, the pencil lines still faintly visible, then turned off the light.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Sara's pressing her hand against the wall most likely shows","choices":["her plan to measure her height again.","that she is checking for damage.","her anger about moving.","her attachment to memories of growing up in the house."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The height marks represent her childhood. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e797f13923","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-231","passage":"<p>When a regional railway added quiet cars, where phone calls and loud conversations were not allowed, officials expected them to appeal mainly to business travelers. Surveys found that the cars were popular with a wide range of passengers, including students studying for exams and parents with sleeping infants. The railway later added a second quiet car to its busiest routes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the railway added a second quiet car most likely because","choices":["demand for quiet space exceeded what one car could provide.","passengers disliked the first quiet car.","phone calls became illegal.","business travelers stopped riding."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Popularity on busy routes led to expansion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8ee19059f7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-234","passage":"<p>When a town replaced a busy four-way stop with a small roundabout, some residents worried that drivers would be confused. For the first few weeks, traffic moved hesitantly. Within months, however, waiting times at the intersection dropped, and the number of collisions fell, as drivers adjusted to yielding while moving slowly through the circle.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the early hesitation most likely resulted from","choices":["drivers being unfamiliar with how to use the roundabout.","broken traffic lights.","an increase in pedestrians.","the roundabout being closed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Drivers later \"adjusted.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.96ab603fe2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-236","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I spent the summer I was fourteen working at my aunt's flower shop, convinced it would be the dullest job imaginable. By August I knew the names of forty flowers, could tie a bow with my eyes closed, and had watched a nervous young man spend twenty minutes choosing roses for a proposal. I had expected to count the days. Instead, I counted the stories.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the anecdote about the young man choosing roses mainly illustrates","choices":["the narrator's plans to become a florist.","how difficult customers can be.","the high price of roses.","the kinds of moments that made the job meaningful to the narrator."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It is one of the \"stories.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6c68a4fda2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-237","passage":"<p>A study of online classes found that students who were required to post short weekly reflections about what they had learned were more likely to finish the course than students in sections without the requirement. Researchers suggest that the reflections kept students engaged and helped instructors notice early when students were struggling.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, instructors reading reflections could most likely","choices":["grade exams automatically.","identify students who needed help before they fell too far behind.","replace the course content.","stop teaching the course."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Reflections helped instructors \"notice early.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3e293a63dc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-240","passage":"<p>Many octopus species live only a year or two and die soon after reproducing. Female octopuses guard their eggs without eating, sometimes for months, until the eggs hatch. Researchers have found that a gland near the octopus's eyes triggers changes that lead to this fasting and decline; when the gland was removed in experiments, mothers abandoned their eggs, resumed eating, and lived longer.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the gland most likely plays a role in","choices":["helping octopuses see in the dark.","causing octopuses to hunt more.","ensuring that mothers stay with their eggs until they hatch.","making eggs hatch faster."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Without it, mothers abandoned eggs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1bcccbbbf1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-241","passage":"<p>When a high school changed its start time from 7:30 to 8:30 a.m., students reported sleeping about forty minutes more on school nights. Attendance in first-period classes improved, and the number of students falling asleep in class decreased. Some parents, however, noted that later dismissal times made it harder to schedule after-school activities and jobs.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, fewer students fell asleep in class most likely because","choices":["teachers stopped giving lectures.","students drank more coffee.","classes became shorter.","they were getting more sleep at night."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They slept about forty minutes more. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.938a0c3162","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-242","passage":"<p>Some art restorers once used strong chemicals to remove old varnish from paintings, occasionally damaging the original paint beneath. Newer methods use gels that release cleaning agents slowly and can be applied to tiny areas under a microscope. These techniques allow restorers to remove grime while preserving delicate brushwork.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, applying cleaning gels under a microscope is most useful because it","choices":["removes the original paint.","lets restorers control exactly where the cleaning agent acts.","speeds up drying time.","makes the painting brighter than it originally was."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They can be applied \"to tiny areas.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.eed2125b20","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-243","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For the whole bus ride, the new girl sat alone, staring out the window. Rosa had planned to sit with her friends, as always. But when the bus stopped at the school, Rosa noticed the girl clutching a map of the building, her hands trembling slightly. Rosa slid into the seat beside her and asked, \"Want me to show you where the gym is?\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the detail about the girl's trembling hands suggests that she","choices":["was cold on the bus.","had been to the school many times.","was angry at Rosa.","felt anxious about her first day."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Trembling while clutching a map shows nervousness. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.71e6f4d5a7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-245","passage":"<p>Scientists studying why some leaves change color in autumn found that as days shorten, trees stop producing green chlorophyll, revealing yellow and orange pigments already present in the leaves. Red colors, however, come from pigments that some trees actively produce in autumn, possibly to protect leaves from sun damage while the trees reabsorb nutrients before the leaves fall.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the red pigments may benefit trees by","choices":["producing chlorophyll.","making leaves fall sooner.","attracting insects to eat the leaves.","helping protect leaves while nutrients are recovered."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Possibly \"to protect leaves from sun damage.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5f16d097c6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-246","passage":"<p>When a grocery chain began placing water bottles next to soda in refrigerated cases at checkout, sales of bottled water increased while soda sales dipped slightly. Previously, water had been stocked only in a distant aisle. The chain's analysts concluded that convenient placement influenced which drink shoppers grabbed at the last moment.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a store hoping to increase sales of fruit might","choices":["hide fruit behind other products.","stop selling soda entirely.","move fruit to the back of the store.","place fruit near the checkout lines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Convenient placement influences quick choices. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3c6ca8cde2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-249","passage":"<p>When a museum began offering sensory-friendly hours, with dimmed lights, quieter galleries, and limited crowds, officials expected to serve mainly visitors with autism. Attendance data showed that many older adults and parents with young children also chose these hours, preferring the calmer atmosphere.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, older adults and parents with young children most likely valued","choices":["louder, busier galleries.","free admission.","a calmer, less crowded environment.","the chance to see new exhibits only."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They preferred \"the calmer atmosphere.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9413c0f533","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-251","passage":"<p>When a public radio station invited listeners to submit short audio stories about their neighborhoods, producers expected a few dozen entries. They received more than a thousand. Many stories came from people who had never spoken on the radio before, and several became among the station's most shared segments online.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the popularity of the segments most likely stemmed partly from","choices":["the stories being fictional.","the fresh, personal perspectives of everyday residents.","the station's decision to shorten news broadcasts.","the stories being read by famous actors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Many came from first-time voices. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d485fac3a1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-252","passage":"<p>Some cities have painted crosswalks with bright, artistic designs. In one city, researchers compared intersections before and after the paintings were added and found that drivers yielded to pedestrians more often at painted crosswalks. The researchers suggest that the eye-catching designs made crosswalks more noticeable.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a crosswalk that drivers often fail to notice might be improved by","choices":["removing its markings.","making it narrower.","making it more visually striking.","painting it the same color as the road."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Visibility led to more yielding. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.977c524136","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-253","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every year on the first snowfall, Grandmother Ana made hot chocolate and told the same story about the winter she met Grandfather. The grandchildren could recite it word for word and pretended to groan. But the year Grandmother was too ill to tell it, the youngest, Pilar, stood up in front of the fire, took a deep breath, and began: \"It was the coldest winter anyone could remember...\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Pilar's decision to tell the story most likely reflects","choices":["her dislike of snow.","an assignment from school.","the family's attachment to the tradition.","her wish to change the story's ending."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She keeps the tradition alive. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.03d7658de6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-254","passage":"<p>When a city began lending air conditioners to elderly residents during heat waves, emergency room visits for heat-related illness among older adults dropped noticeably. Social workers delivering the units also checked on residents' health and connected several with other services, such as meal delivery, that they had not known about.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, delivering the air conditioners in person was valuable partly because it","choices":["prevented heat waves.","allowed social workers to identify other needs residents had.","made the air conditioners work better.","replaced the need for meal delivery."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Social workers connected residents with services. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2439bc4118","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-255","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every morning, Omar's father left a folded note in his lunch bag, usually a joke so bad that Omar groaned aloud in the cafeteria. By high school, Omar pretended to be embarrassed and threw the notes away before his friends could see. Years later, packing to leave for college, he found a shoebox in his father's closet filled with copies of every note, each one dated in careful handwriting.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the careful dating of each note suggests that Omar's father","choices":["needed the notes for work.","forgot which jokes he had written.","planned to publish the jokes.","treasured the notes as a record of his son's childhood."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Dated copies show care. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ac32d5ef66","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-256","passage":"<p>Researchers studying wild chimpanzees observed that groups in different regions use different tools and techniques, such as cracking nuts with stones in some areas but not in others where the same nuts and stones are available. Because these differences are not explained by the environment alone, some researchers describe them as cultural traditions passed down through learning.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, young chimpanzees most likely acquire their group's tool techniques by","choices":["copying chimpanzees from distant regions.","inventing them independently each generation.","inheriting them without any learning.","learning from others in their group."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Traditions are \"passed down through learning.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.40a25f5ff3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-262","passage":"<p>When a town created a community tool library where residents could borrow ladders, drills, and garden equipment, organizers expected mainly homeowners to use it. Many borrowers turned out to be renters who lacked storage space and did not want to buy tools they would use only occasionally. The library also became a place where neighbors shared repair tips.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, renters found the tool library especially useful because they","choices":["were required to borrow tools.","had limited space and rarely needed tools.","owned more tools than homeowners.","wanted to sell their tools."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"lacked storage space\" and used tools occasionally. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d6791ef90e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-265","passage":"<p>Scientists studying a species of songbird found that birds living on islands with fewer predators had duller feathers than birds of the same species on the mainland. Bright colors can attract mates but also make birds easier for predators to spot. The researchers suggest that the balance between attracting mates and avoiding predators differs between the two environments.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which factor would researchers most likely investigate to explain why island birds became duller despite having fewer predators?","choices":["Whether predators prefer dull birds","Whether island birds eat different seeds only","Whether mainland birds live longer","Whether bright colors provide less benefit for attracting mates on the islands"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fewer predators alone would favor brightness, so mate attraction may differ. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c8bf64d90b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-266","passage":"<p>When a public library began offering free museum passes that patrons could reserve, the passes were booked months in advance. Surveys found that many families who used them had never visited those museums because of ticket costs. Several museums reported that some pass users later returned as paying visitors or members.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the museums most likely benefited from the program by","choices":["charging families higher prices.","closing on weekends.","gaining new visitors who might continue to visit.","selling fewer memberships."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Some pass users returned as paying visitors or members. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8ab4d827c5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-271","passage":"<p>When a small city replaced its paper parking tickets with a smartphone payment system, officials expected mainly convenience benefits. They also discovered that the data showed which blocks were almost always full and which were rarely used. The city then adjusted prices block by block, raising them where spaces were scarce and lowering them where spaces sat empty, and drivers found open spaces more easily.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, lowering prices on rarely used blocks was most likely intended to","choices":["close those blocks to parking.","encourage drivers to park in spaces that were usually empty.","force drivers to use paper tickets.","reduce the city's revenue."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cheaper spaces spread demand. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.b114ef4fa3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-273","passage":"<p>Biologists found that some trees in dry regions shed their leaves during drought and quickly regrow them after rain, while other trees keep their leaves but close tiny pores to conserve water. Trees that drop their leaves avoid water loss almost entirely but cannot produce food until they regrow leaves. Trees that keep leaves can resume food production immediately but still lose some water.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, after a brief rain in the middle of a long drought, which trees would most likely begin producing food first?","choices":["Trees that dropped their leaves","Both types at exactly the same time","Trees that kept their leaves","Neither type"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Leaf keepers \"can resume food production immediately.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8e1f69e33d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-276","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I learned patience from my grandmother's bread. She would mix the dough and then do nothing at all for two hours, reading or knitting while it rose. As a child I found this unbearable and would lift the towel every ten minutes to check. \"Watching doesn't make it grow faster,\" she'd say, and pull the towel back down.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the narrator repeatedly lifted the towel as a child because she","choices":["thought the dough was burning.","wanted to add more flour.","was impatient to see the dough rise.","was helping her grandmother knead."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She \"found this unbearable.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c7ad2f979a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-279","passage":"<p>When a community garden added raised beds at wheelchair height, organizers hoped to include gardeners with disabilities. The beds proved popular with many older gardeners as well, who found them easier on their backs and knees. The garden later replaced several ground-level plots with raised beds because of demand.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, older gardeners preferred the raised beds most likely because they","choices":["needed no watering.","produced more vegetables.","required less bending and kneeling.","were located indoors."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Easier \"on their backs and knees.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f751bdd91b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-280","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>Each autumn my mother pressed a leaf / between the pages of whatever book she read, / so that years later, opening a novel, / I'd find a maple, flat and red, / marking not a chapter / but a season of her life, / a small dry letter / from a year I barely knew.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the speaker most likely feels that the leaves","choices":["offer glimpses into parts of her mother's past.","damage the books.","are meaningless decorations.","were placed there by the speaker."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"A small dry letter from a year I barely knew.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6c05734378","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-282","passage":"<p>When a town's historic movie theater faced closure, residents formed a nonprofit and raised money through small donations to buy the building. They showed older films, hosted local music performances, and rented the space for community events. Within five years, the theater was financially stable and had become a gathering place for the town.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the theater's variety of events most likely helped it survive by","choices":["relying on a single donor.","reducing the number of visitors.","focusing only on film.","attracting different audiences and sources of income."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Films, music, and rentals diversified use. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3775e6c9c6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-283","passage":"<p>Scientists studying desert tortoises found that the animals dig burrows that can stay cool even when surface temperatures are extremely high. Other species, including snakes, lizards, and insects, often use abandoned tortoise burrows to escape the heat. The researchers describe the tortoises as important to the survival of many other desert animals.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a decline in desert tortoises would most likely","choices":["reduce shelter available to other desert animals.","have no effect on other species.","increase the number of cool burrows.","cause surface temperatures to fall."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Many animals rely on their burrows. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8a7f7d693e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-284","passage":"<p>When a hospital began allowing family members to stay overnight with patients in the intensive care unit, some staff worried that visitors would interfere with care. Instead, nurses reported that families often noticed changes in patients' conditions and alerted staff early. Patients and families also reported lower stress levels.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, families were helpful partly because they","choices":["performed medical procedures.","prevented doctors from visiting.","replaced nurses at night.","knew patients well enough to notice changes."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They \"noticed changes ... and alerted staff.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c900a225fe","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-285","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Leo had practiced his speech for the class election until he knew every word. At the podium, he looked out at his classmates, and the words vanished. After a long, awful pause, he said the only thing he could think of: \"I forgot my speech. But I remember what I wanted to say.\" Then he told them, plainly, why he wanted to help. The room was quiet when he finished, and then it applauded.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the classmates' applause most likely suggests that they","choices":["preferred memorized speeches.","did not hear the speech.","were relieved the speech was over.","appreciated Leo's sincerity."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Plain honesty earned applause. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0fac1ad3b3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-287","passage":"<p>When a city offered free public Wi-Fi in parks, officials expected people to use it mainly for entertainment. Surveys found that many users were students completing homework and job seekers submitting applications, especially in neighborhoods where many households lacked home internet.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the Wi-Fi program was likely most valuable in neighborhoods where","choices":["there were no parks.","many households did not have internet at home.","students had no homework.","every household had fast internet."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Use was highest where home internet was lacking. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.7df8e66583","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-288","passage":"<p>Ornithologists studying crows in a city found that the birds dropped hard-shelled nuts onto crosswalks and waited for cars to crack them. The crows then retrieved the nut pieces when traffic lights stopped the cars. Researchers noted that the birds appeared to time their retrieval to the light signals.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the crows most likely retrieve nut pieces when lights stop traffic because","choices":["drivers feed them at stoplights.","it is safer to walk on the road when cars are not moving.","the lights attract insects.","the nuts crack only at red lights."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Stopped cars reduce danger. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9f226915ef","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-289","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father could fix almost anything, but he rarely explained how. When I was twelve and the lawnmower broke, he handed me the manual and a screwdriver and went inside. I spent the entire afternoon covered in grease, furious. By evening the mower started. He came out, listened to it run, nodded once, and went back in. I have never been prouder of a nod.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the father's decision to go inside most likely reflects","choices":["his wish to call a repair service.","his intention to let the narrator solve the problem independently.","his lack of interest in the lawnmower.","his belief that the mower could not be fixed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He left her with tools and the manual. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e4cdf1a7bb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-291","passage":"<p>A study of museum visitors found that people who took photographs of artworks remembered fewer details about them later than people who simply looked. However, when visitors zoomed in on a specific part of an artwork while photographing it, their memory for that part improved. Researchers suggest that focused attention, not photography itself, affects memory.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a visitor who wants to remember a painting's details while taking photos should most likely","choices":["deliberately focus attention on specific parts of the painting.","avoid looking at the painting.","photograph only the frame.","take many quick photos without looking."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Focused attention improves memory. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6b486319b7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-292","passage":"<p>Researchers studying why some neighborhoods have more street trees than others found that wealthier areas often had greater tree cover, partly because homeowners could afford to plant and maintain trees. In response, several cities launched programs to plant trees in neighborhoods with low tree cover and to fund their care during the first few years.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, cities funded tree care during the first few years most likely because","choices":["mature trees need no care.","residents asked cities to remove trees.","young trees require care that residents may not be able to afford.","trees are cheaper to plant than to maintain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Maintenance costs had limited tree cover. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e00f440957","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-296","passage":"<p>When a school district replaced sugary drinks in vending machines with water and milk, researchers expected students might simply buy sodas elsewhere. Surveys found that overall soda consumption among students did decline, though some students did bring drinks from home. Consumption fell most among younger students, who had fewer opportunities to buy drinks off campus.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, older students' soda consumption likely fell less because they","choices":["had more chances to buy drinks away from school.","were not surveyed.","preferred milk.","did not use vending machines."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Younger students \"had fewer opportunities to buy drinks off campus.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.29a51443f6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-299","passage":"<p>When a city introduced a program allowing residents to report potholes through a phone app with photos and locations, repair crews could plan efficient routes and fix clusters of potholes in a single trip. Average repair times fell, and residents reported greater satisfaction with road maintenance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, repair crews became more efficient mainly because they","choices":["received fewer reports.","stopped repairing some roads.","worked longer hours.","could see where potholes were clustered and plan trips accordingly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fixing \"clusters of potholes in a single trip.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.336039095b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-300","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandmother's hands / were never still: / shelling peas, / mending hems, / braiding my hair / tight enough to last a week. / Even in the hospital, / her fingers moved across the blanket, / looking for something to do.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the image of the grandmother's fingers \"looking for something to do\" suggests that","choices":["being useful was deeply part of who she was.","she was trying to call a nurse.","she wanted to leave the hospital.","she had forgotten how to sew."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A lifetime of busy hands persists. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c0b15b151d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-301","passage":"<p>When a city planted wildflower meadows along highway medians instead of mowed grass, maintenance crews mowed only once a year instead of every few weeks. The city saved money on fuel and labor, and surveys of the medians found many more bees and butterflies than before. Some drivers initially complained that the medians looked overgrown, but complaints declined as the flowers bloomed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, drivers' complaints most likely declined because","choices":["drivers stopped using the highway.","the wildflowers were removed.","the blooming flowers made the medians look more attractive.","the city mowed the medians more often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Complaints fell \"as the flowers bloomed.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.da37679995","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-302","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For weeks, Nadia's little brother had begged to come along on her early-morning runs, and for weeks she had said no. He was too slow, she told him. One Saturday she finally gave in. He fell behind within a block, red-faced and panting, but he did not stop. At the end of the route, Nadia found herself jogging in place, waiting, and cheering louder than she ever had for herself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Nadia's cheering \"louder than she ever had for herself\" suggests that she","choices":["wanted her brother to stop running.","was cheering for another runner.","felt proud of her brother's effort.","was annoyed at having to wait."],"answer":"C","explanation":"His determination inspires her pride. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
